</code></pre></p>

Aspect Interface (Declaration) Implementation (Definition) What What function expects How function works Contains Signature only Full body Changes Rarely Can change freely Client sees Yes No (hidden) <p>Example: <pre><code>// Interface (header file)
int f2(int x, int y);

// Implementation A
int f2(int x, int y) { return x + y; }

// Implementation B (same interface)
int f2(int x, int y) { 
    int temp = x + y; 
    return temp; 
}
</code></pre> Client code compiles against declaration; implementation can change without recompiling client.</p>

Expression What Happens <code>(*p)++</code> Increment value at <code>p</code> (old value returned) <code>*p++</code> Increment pointer <code>p</code> (old value returned) <pre><code>int a[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
int *p = a;
for(int i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i)
    printf("%d ", *p++);  // Print a[i], then advance p
</code></pre> <p>Why <code>*p++</code> works: Post-increment on pointer, then dereference old address.</p>

Operation Pointer <code>p</code> Array <code>a</code> <code>p = a</code> ✓ N/A <code>p++</code> ✓ ✗ (constant pointer) <code>a++</code> N/A ✗ Error! <pre><code>int a[] = {10, 20, 30};
int *p = a;
// a++  // ERROR: array is constant pointer
p++;   // OK
</code></pre>

Design Links Enqueue Dequeue 1. Reverse Rear → ... → Front O(1) O(n) traverse from rear 2. Forward Front → ... → Rear O(1) O(1) <p>Rule: Singly linked list → front must point to first node, each node points to next arrival.</p> <pre><code>struct node { int key_; struct node* next_; };
typedef struct node node_t;

struct queue {
    node_t* front_;  // First to leave
    node_t* rear_;   // Last to leave
};
typedef struct queue queue_t;
</code></pre>
